# lint/drift-allowlist — services whose OWN lint config disagrees with kit's.
#
#   diverged <repo> <path> <enable|disable|formatters> \
#             reason="..." owner=<who> since=YYYY-MM-DD until=YYYY-MM-DD
#
# WHAT THIS IS FOR
#   The drift check in tests/validate.sh compares every service repo that calls
#   the reusable workflow against `lint/golangci.yml` and reports the
#   DIFFERENCE — linter by linter — rather than failing on the mere presence of
#   a file. That comparison is the thing that ends the failure this packet
#   exists to end: a copied config has no propagation, so it rots silently.
#
#   This file is where a difference that is KNOWN and OWNED is recorded, because
#   kit does not fix other people's repositories and cannot. Every line here is
#   a debt with a name on it and a date it comes due.
#
# WHY IT IS NOT "MUST NOT EXIST"
#   A service whose `.golangci.yml` is byte-identical to kit's has not drifted;
#   it has reached the same policy by another route. Failing that would teach the
#   lesson that kit's config is something you get shouted at for owning, which
#   is how a standard stops being adopted. `tests/self_test.sh` breakage 27b
#   asserts this as a GREEN case for exactly that reason.
#
# FOUR RULES, and the fourth is the one that earns the other three
#   1. reason, owner, since and until are all required.
#   2. An EXPIRED entry FAILS on the day it expires. The gate reads the clock.
#   3. A duplicate (repo, path, key) FAILS — one of the two is not being read.
#   4. AN ENTRY THAT NO LONGER DESCRIBES A REAL DIFFERENCE FAILS.
#
#   Rule 4 is modelled on ESLint's `reportUnusedDisableDirectives`, which
#   reports a disable comment that no longer suppresses anything. Without it an
#   allowlist is a ratchet that only turns one way: the repository gets fixed,
#   the entry stays, the entry stops meaning anything, and within two quarters
#   this file contains every repository the fleet has ever had. The unused entry
#   is how you find out first — and it is the rule that makes the other three
#   worth having.
#
# ONE LINE PER ENTRY. A wrapped reason is two entries, one of which is a parse
# error, which is a confusing way to learn that a file is one line per row.

# --- identity ---------------------------------------------------------------
# It has a `.golangci.yml` that enables NO linter at all: it exists solely to
# exclude one 10,000-line generated file, and its own header says so. That is a
# defensible decision and a real deviation — under this packet's mechanism,
# identity's lint step passes `--config` at kit's file, so a repo-root config of
# its own is not a supported state any more.
#
# The migration is identity's to make and is not kit's to do. It is recorded
# here, with an owner, rather than being either ignored or treated as a
# permanently red gate.
#
# What the split actually is, measured rather than assumed: `--config` WINS, so
# this file is INERT for kit's CI — `golangci-lint run -v` names kit's config and
# only kit's. The problem is not that it hijacks the build. It is that it does
# not, and a developer's own `golangci-lint run` in that repository reads THIS
# file, so identity enforces a five-linter default set locally and kit's policy
# in CI, and nothing on the page says the two disagree.
diverged identity .golangci.yml enable reason="the file exists only to exclude client/generated/api.gen.go and enables nothing; migrating it onto kit's policy is identity's change, not kit's" owner=identity since=2026-09-30 until=2026-12-31
diverged identity .golangci.yml formatters reason="same file, same migration: gofmt and goimports come from kit's config once identity stops carrying one of its own" owner=identity since=2026-09-30 until=2026-12-31
